  "configuration_id": "config_005",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: All four completed experiments use singleprofile with no aggregation. We know RNA (scgpt) dominates (0.4106 rna_only vs 0.2266 image_only), that multimodal concat fusion (config_006, 0.4348) modestly beats weighted (config_001, 0.4224), and that the image modality adds only ~2% over RNA alone. However, the profile-setting axis (single vs. multi) is completely unexplored, representing the largest remaining source of architectural uncertainty. 2) What this tests: config_005 (multimodal, multiprofile, scgpt, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, concat) is the minimal-change extension of our best result (config_006) to the multiprofile regime—only the profile setting and the required aggregation (avgpool) change, while keeping the same RNA encoder, image encoder, and concat fusion. This cleanly isolates whether multiple profiles with average-pool aggregation improve over a single profile. 3) Why it is the best next experiment: The profile/aggregation axis is the highest-uncertainty, highest-impact design question remaining. Testing it with the simplest aggregation (avgpool) and holding all other variables at their best-known values (scgpt, imagenet_vit_l, concat) follows the principle of changing as few factors as possible. A positive result would open the multiprofile design space for further exploitation; a null or negative result would justify concentrating the remaining budget on singleprofile refinements (e.g., RNA encoder choice, image encoder choice). Either outcome substantially reduces uncertainty about a core architectural decision under a tight budget."
}

